## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design modular, policy-driven prompt systems that scale across tenants, balance latency with safety, and provide auditable provenance.

## Key Concepts

- Modular prompt blocks
- Policy graph
- Per-tenant constraints
- Latency-aware composition
- Determinism and reproducibility
- Provenance and audit

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch (pseudo-implementation)
function composePrompt(intent, blockLibrary, policy){
  const allowed = blockLibrary.filter(b => policy.allows(b.tag, intent));
  const prompt = allowed.map(b => b.template).join("\n");
  const audit = { intent, blocks: allowed.map(b => b.id), provenance: policy.provenance };
  return { prompt, audit };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate determinism when blocks or transformations are nondeterministic?
- How would policy graph evolution affect audit integrity and prompt reproducibility?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design end-to-end prompt orchestration under SLA; covers multi-model choice, caching, and budget-based pruning in a multi-tenant context.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic plan generation with per-model latency and cost profiles
- Content-addressable caching to reduce repeated work
- Budget-aware pruning and graceful degradation
- Tenant isolation and guardrails in orchestration

## Code Example

```javascript
class MockModel {
  constructor(name, tokenCost, latencyMs){
    this.name = name; this.tokenCost = tokenCost; this.latencyMs = latencyMs;
  }
  run(prompt){
    return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve(`${this.name}-out:${prompt.slice(0,60)}`), this.latencyMs));
  }
}

const modelA = new MockModel('ModelA', 0.5, 120);
const modelB = new MockModel('ModelB', 0.2, 60);
const cache = new Map();
function hash(s){ let h=0; for(let i=0;i<s.length;i++){ h=(h<<5)-h+s.charCodeAt(i); h|=0;} return h.toString(); }
function plan(intent){ return [ [modelA, intent+" [segment1]"], [modelB, intent+" [segment2]"] ]; }
async function runPlan(intent, budgetTokens, maxLatencyMs=300){
  const key = hash(intent);
  if(cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);
  const steps = plan(intent);
  let totalLatency = 0; const outputs = [];
  for(const [m,p] of steps){ const out = await m.run(p); outputs.push(out); totalLatency += m.latencyMs; if(totalLatency>maxLatencyMs) break; }
  const result = outputs.join(' | ');
  cache.set(key, result);
  return result;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Designing a modular, policy-driven prompt assembly system tests understanding of multi-tenant safety, latency budgets, and deterministic prompt composition under constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Fragment catalogs with metadata: policy, latency, locale, tone
- Tenant-aware scoring and ordering under latency limits
- Guardrails and watermark injection when needed
- Audit logging with fragment IDs and decision trace

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype outline (not full implementation)
const fragments = [
  {id:'intent', text:'Explain concisely', latency:10},
  {id:'tone', text:'in a formal style', latency:5},
  {id:'locale', text:'English', latency:2}
];
function assemble(tenantPolicy, budget){
  // simplistic greedy selection
  let chosen = [];
  let used= new Set();
  for(const f of fragments){
    if(!used.has(f.id) && budget- f.latency >=0 && tenantPolicy.includes(f.id)){
      chosen.push(f); used.add(f.id);
      budget -= f.latency;
    }
    if(chosen.length>=3) break;
  }
  return chosen.map(x=>x.text).join(' ');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle non-determinism in fragment selection across model updates?
- How to validate audits for compliance and GDPR logging obligations?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to design scalable, per-tenant prompt pipelines with strict latency constraints and privacy guarantees. This tests modular design, policy enforcement, and observability.

## Key Concepts
- Modular prompt construction from a registry
- Per-tenant policy engine and data isolation
- Guard injection and data minimization
- Latency budgeting and deterministic composition
- Auditability and observability

## Code Example
```python
# Minimal skeleton illustrating components
class ModuleRegistry:
    def __init__(self):
        self._modules = {}
    def register(self, name, fn):
        self._modules[name] = fn
    def build(self, order, base=""):
        prompt = base
        for name in order:
            prompt += self._modules[name](prompt)
        return prompt

class GuardInjector:
    def inject(self, prompt, policy):
        guard = policy.get("guard", "")
        return f"{guard}\n{prompt}"

class PolicyEngine:
    def __init__(self, policies):
        self.policies = policies
    def select_order(self, tenant, latency_budget_ms):
        policy = self.policies.get(tenant, {})
        return policy.get("module_order", []), policy

